<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/software/polkit/policyconfig-1.dtd">
<!--
  Example polkit action for idunn's interactive elevation on Linux (design.md
  §14.2.2, backlog IDN-08). Install it as
  /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/<your reverse-DNS id>.policy, owned by root, and
  replace the action id, vendor, messages and helper path with your own.

  It is optional. Without it pkexec uses its generic action
  (org.freedesktop.policykit.exec), which already demands administrator
  authentication; what this file adds is a message that names your application.

  exec.path must be the helper's path with every symlink resolved: that is the
  path idunn hands to pkexec (elevate.InteractiveOptions.HelperPath), and it
  has to lie in directories only root can write or idunn refuses to elevate it.
  If the helper's resolved path changes with every version (a helper inside a
  version directory), point exec.path at a stable, root-owned helper instead —
  or ship no policy and let the generic action apply.

  Choices made here, deliberately:

    * auth_admin, not auth_admin_keep. "keep" lets polkit remember the
      authorization for a few minutes, so a second request in that window runs
      the helper as root with no dialog at all. The dialog is the user's
      decision about this apply; an update is rare enough that asking each time
      costs little, and a remembered "yes" is exactly what a second, unrelated
      request would ride on. A vendor who wants "keep" should make that
      decision knowingly, not inherit it from an example.
    * No allow_active="yes". That would let any logged-in user install into a
      root that is root-owned precisely because not everyone may write it.
    * No org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.argv1. The arguments are the request;
      idunn validates them before pkexec starts, and the helper validates them
      again (elevate.AcceptRequest). Moving that judgement into a policy file
      adds a third place to keep in step, not a defence.
    * No org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui. The helper has no window and
      needs no display variables.
-->
<policyconfig>
  <vendor>Example</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://example.com/</vendor_url>

  <action id="com.example.acme.apply-update">
    <description>Install an update for Acme</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to install an update for Acme.</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/opt/acme/bin/acme-helper</annotate>
  </action>
</policyconfig>
